              {
                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Three gray (5) lines on black (0). Output: same grid, lines recolored. Longest -> blue (1), middle -> yellow (4), shortest -> red (2).\n\nWait let me check: verifier: x1 = merge(objects) = all gray cells. x2 = fill(I, FOUR, x1) => all gray become 4 (yellow). Then x3 = argmin = smallest object, x6 = fill(x2, TWO, x3) -> smallest becomes 2 (red). x4 = argmax = largest, x5 = fill(x2, ONE, x4) -> largest becomes 1 (blue). Middle stays 4 (yellow).\n\nCheck example: lines. Column 0 has 5 at rows 0-2 (length 3). Column 9 has 5 at rows 0-6 (length 7). Column 3 has 5 at rows 2-4 (length 3)? Wait let's read.\n\nInput:\nRow0: 5 at col0, col9\nRow1: 5 at col0, col9\nRow2: 5 at col0, col3, col9\nRow3: 5 at col0, col3, col9\nRow4: 5 at col3, col9  (col0 not)\nRow5: 5 at col9\nRow6: 5 at col9\n\nColumn 0: rows 0,1,2,3 = length 4.\nColumn 3: rows 2,3,4 = length 3.\nColumn 9: rows 0-6 = length 7.\n\nOutput: col9 = 1 (longest, blue). col0 = 4 (middle length 4, yellow). col3 = 2 (shortest, red). Yes matches.\n\nSo the task: three gray lines of differing lengths, horizontal or vertical? Example has vertical lines. But lines could be horizontal too. The rule says \"lines\". In general, objects are connected gray components (4-connectivity). Recolor by size: largest blue(1), middle yellow(4), smallest red(2).\n\nNow I need to create 4 demonstration pairs. I should make them diverse, showing horizontal and vertical lines, different lengths, different grid sizes, positions.\n\nImportant: the solver must infer rule from these 4 pairs. Should be clear. Let me design inputs with exactly 3 gray objects (lines), distinguished by length.\n\nThe lines must be gray (5), background black (0). Make lines straight, axis-aligned, of three distinct lengths.\n\nLet me create varied examples.\n\nExample 1: vertical lines, grid 10x10 similar to example but different arrangement. Actually I should be careful: any grid where verifier works. Let me just make valid grids.\n\nLet me design:\n\nPair A: grid 8x8. Vertical lines:\n- col1 rows 0-6 length 7 (longest)\n- col4 rows 1-3 length 3 (shortest)\n- col6 rows 0-4 length 5 (middle)\nOutput: col1 ->1, col6 ->4, col4 ->2.\n\nPair B: horizontal lines, grid 7x9.\n- row1 cols 0-2 length 3 (shortest)\n- row3 cols 0-6 length 7 (longest)\n- row5 cols 1-5 length 5 (middle)\nOutput: row3 ->1, row5->4, row1->2.\n\nPair C: mix? Maybe all vertical or horizontal. But could include a diagonal line? Connected component regardless of shape. Rule doesn't require straight lines really\u2014it's about object sizes. But natural language says lines. Keep lines.\n\nActually to make the rule inferable, maybe show that color depends on size rank. Provide different lengths and mixed orientations.\n\nPair C: grid 6x6, vertical:\n- col0 rows 0-1 length 2 (shortest)\n- col2 rows 0-4 length 5 (longest)\n- col5 rows 1-3 length 3 (middle)\nOutput: col2->1, col5->4, col0->2.\n\nPair D: grid 5x10, horizontal:\n- row0 cols 0-8 length 9 (longest)\n- row2 cols 2-4 length 3 (shortest)\n- row4 cols 1-5 length 5... wait need 3 lines, distinct.\n\nLet me make grid 5x10:\n- row0 cols 0-8 (9)\n- row2 cols 3-5 (3)\n- row4 cols 1-6 (6)\nOutput: row0->1, row4->4, row2->2.\n\nNow verify the verifier would handle. objects(I,T,F,T): univalued true, diagonal false, without_bg true. Background mostcolor = 0. Each line separate component. Good.\n\nI should call show_transformed_input for each. Let me build the grids as matrices.\n\nPair A: 8x8\ncol1 rows0-6, col4 rows1-3, col6 rows0-4.\n\nRows:\nr0: col1=5, col6=5 -> [0,5,0,0,0,0,5,0]\nr1: col1=5, col4=5, col6=5 -> [0,5,0,0,5,0,5,0]\nr2: same as r1 -> [0,5,0,0,5,0,5,0]\nr3: same -> [0,5,0,0,5,0,5,0]\nr4: col1=5, col6=5 -> [0,5,0,0,0,0,5,0]\nr5: col1=5, col6=5 -> [0,5,0,0,0,0,5,0]\nr6: col1=5, col6=5 -> [0,5,0,0,0,0,5,0]\nr7: all 0.\n\nCheck col1: rows0-6 length7. col4: rows1-3 length3. col6: rows0-4 length5. Good.\n\nPair B: 7x9\nrow1 cols0-2, row3 cols0-6, row5 cols1-5.\nr0: all 0 (9 zeros)\nr1: [5,5,5,0,0,0,0,0,0]\nr2: zeros\nr3: [5,5,5,5,5,5,5,0,0]\nr4: zeros\nr5: [0,5,5,5,5,5,0,0,0]\nr6: zeros\n\nPair C: 6x6\ncol0 rows0-1, col2 rows0-4, col5 rows1-3.\nr0: col0=5, col2=5 -> [5,0,5,0,0,0]\nr1: col0=5, col2=5, col5=5 -> [5,0,5,0,0,5]\nr2: col2=5, col5=5 -> [0,0,5,0,0,5]\nr3: col2=5, col5=5 -> [0,0,5,0,0,5]\nr4: col2=5 -> [0,0,5,0,0,0]\nr5: zeros\n\ncol0 length2, col2 length5, col5 length3. Good.\n\nPair D: 5x10\nrow0 cols0-8, row2 cols3-5, row4 cols1-6.\nr0: [5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,0]\nr1: zeros\nr2: [0,0,0,5,5,5,0,0,0,0]\nr3: zeros\nr4: [0,5,5,5,5,5,5,0,0,0]\n\nLengths: row0=9, row2=3, row4=6. Good.\n\nLet me submit all four in parallel.\n\nWait\u2014one concern: does the rule rely on 3 objects; if a line touches edge it's fine. All good.\n\nLet me call.",
